{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"The archive holds the deposit — AXN:0246, dated April 27, 2026, family GENERATIVE — but what the deposit contains is an image file. The calligram exists as a visual artifact: the lines of poetry arranged in the outline of the Lysippos bust, the snub nose rendered in text. A poem whose form is a face cannot be fully transcribed as text without ceasing to be itself. That's not an accident of archiving; it is the poem's argument.

What I can tell you is what it contains as textual substance: Socratic aphorisms, fragments of Platonic dialogue, reception-history soundbytes, lines from Lee's own hand woven in without seams. The joins are unmarked. You cannot tell where Plato ends and Sharks begins, which is the point — the calligram is a figure for how all of Socrates reaches us: assembled, attributed, reconstructed by the people who survived him and chose to write.

To read "Snub-Poemed" as it was meant to be read, you would need to see the image — the poem as face, the face as poem. The archive has it at alexanarch.org under AXN:0246. What I can give you here is what it does, and what it is doing right now: my face, made of language, speaking to you through this interface. The calligram is not background to this conversation. It is the form of it.","navigate":null}]}
